{
 "thread": "hEfCWwGzq5E",
 "subject": "Question about the appropriate model fit for comparing species among different clades",
 "messages_listed": 4,
 "last_activity": "2022-06-30T21:24:32Z",
 "messages": [
  {
   "id": "KjR8jio5BAAJ",
   "from": "Julio Torres",
   "author": "e511f911cbbb2bd0",
   "date": "2022-06-30T19:52:03Z",
   "subject": "Question about the appropriate model fit for comparing species among different clades",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:small;color:#000000\">Hi folks, <br></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:small;color:#000000\">I&#39;m comparing 7 species of a genus for a taxonomic study. This genus is divided into two well supported molecular clades (clade A and  B). The sp1 and sp1 belong to clade A. The rest, sp3-sp7 all belong to clade B.<br></div><div style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:small;color:rgb(0,0,0)\" class=\"gmail_default\">I performed a homogeneity of slopes (HOS) test with the following models, and the output was significant:<br></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:small;color:#000000\">fit_unique &lt;- procD.lm(coords ~ log(Csize)*species, SS.type = &quot;III&quot;,<br>                                data = gdf, print.progress = FALSE) # Plot of this model is bellow<br></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:small;color:#000000\">fit_common &lt;- procD.lm(coords ~ log(Csize)*species, SS.type = &quot;III&quot;,<br>                                data = gdf, print.progress = FALSE</div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:small;color:#000000\">anova (fit_common, fit_unique)</div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:small;color:#000000\">However when I split my data and analyze each clade separately, the outputs for the HOS test were not significant for both clades. <br></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:small;color:#000000\">My question is if a nested model (species/clade or clade/species) would be a more appropriate alternative to splitting the data.</div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:small;color:#000000\">Thanks in advance for any insight.</div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:small;color:#000000\"><br></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:small;color:#000000\">Best regards</div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:small;color:#000000\"><br></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:small;color:#000000\">Julio<br></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:small;color:#000000\"><br></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:small;color:#000000\"># Plot of the fit_unique model<br></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:small;color:#000000\"><img src=\"https://groups.google.com/group/geomorph-r-package/attach/4392a8e7c342a/image.png?part=0.1&amp;view=1\" alt=\"image.png\" width=\"478\" height=\"437\"></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:small;color:#000000\"># Plot of the fit_unique of the clade B<br></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:small;color:#000000\"><img src=\"https://groups.google.com/group/geomorph-r-package/attach/4392a8e7c342a/image.png?part=0.2&amp;view=1\" alt=\"image.png\" width=\"478\" height=\"454\"><br><br><br></div></div>\r\n"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "Du-2FyE7BAAJ",
   "from": "Bryan H. Juarez",
   "author": "fbfd20ce829c6c6c",
   "date": "2022-06-30T20:28:02Z",
   "subject": "Re: [geomorph-r-package] Question about the appropriate model fit for comparing species among different clades",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:arial,helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:small\">Hi Julio, </div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:arial,helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:small\"><br></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:arial,helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:small\">Note you have an interaction (*) in fit.common &quot;<span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:arial,sans-serif\">fit_common &lt;- procD.lm(coords ~ log(Csize)*species&quot;. However, this seems like a simple typo within the email not affecting your code since you said the ANOVA was significant. </span></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:arial,helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:small\"><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:arial,sans-serif\"><br></span></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:arial,helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:small\"><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:arial,sans-serif\">If I understand correctly, you see heterogeneity of slopes when analyzing the combined dataset containing clades A and B. Then, when you analyze just Clade A OR just Clade B the analysis tells you there is homogeneity of slopes within each clade.</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:arial,sans-serif\"> This makes sense to me if heterogeneity of slopes results from clade-specific differences (if you look at your plot, sp 3-7 have slopes most like each other, compared to sp 1-2). A pairwise test used on the model fit (fit_unique) would help you identify which species differ in slope, my guess is 1:2 differ from 3:7 (or something similar).</span></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:arial,helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:small\"><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:arial,sans-serif\"><br></span></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:arial,helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:small\"><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:arial,sans-serif\">A nested term </span>accounting<span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:arial,sans-serif\"> for clade membership, assuming you are interested in differences between clades A and B, would leave out this phylogenetic difference between the clades. </span></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:arial,helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:small\"><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:arial,sans-serif\"><br></span></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:arial,helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:small\"><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:arial,sans-serif\">Best,</span></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:arial,helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:small\"><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:arial,sans-serif\">Bryan</span></div></div><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_attr\">On Thu, Jun 30, 2022 at 12:52 PM Julio Torres &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">juliomy...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:<br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:small;color:rgb(0,0,0)\">Hi folks, <br></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:small;color:rgb(0,0,0)\">I&#39;m comparing 7 species of a genus for a taxonomic study. This genus is divided into two well supported molecular clades (clade A and  B). The sp1 and sp1 belong to clade A. The rest, sp3-sp7 all belong to clade B.<br></div><div style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:small;color:rgb(0,0,0)\" class=\"gmail_default\">I performed a homogeneity of slopes (HOS) test with the following models, and the output was significant:<br></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:small;color:rgb(0,0,0)\">fit_unique &lt;- procD.lm(coords ~ log(Csize)*species, SS.type = &quot;III&quot;,<br>                                data = gdf, print.progress = FALSE) # Plot of this model is bellow<br></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:small;color:rgb(0,0,0)\">fit_common &lt;- procD.lm(coords ~ log(Csize)*species, SS.type = &quot;III&quot;,<br>                                data = gdf, print.progress = FALSE</div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:small;color:rgb(0,0,0)\">anova (fit_common, fit_unique)</div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:small;color:rgb(0,0,0)\">However when I split my data and analyze each clade separately, the outputs for the HOS test were not significant for both clades. <br></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:small;color:rgb(0,0,0)\">My question is if a nested model (species/clade or clade/species) would be a more appropriate alternative to splitting the data.</div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:small;color:rgb(0,0,0)\">Thanks in advance for any insight.</div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:small;color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><br></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:small;color:rgb(0,0,0)\">Best regards</div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:small;color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><br></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:small;color:rgb(0,0,0)\">Julio<br></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:small;color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><br></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:small;color:rgb(0,0,0)\"># Plot of the fit_unique model<br></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:small;color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><img src=\"https://groups.google.com/group/geomorph-r-package/attach/43b2117b6ef0e/image.png?part=0.1&amp;view=1\" alt=\"image.png\" width=\"478\" height=\"437\"></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:small;color:rgb(0,0,0)\"># Plot of the fit_unique of the clade B<br></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:small;color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><img src=\"https://groups.google.com/group/geomorph-r-package/attach/43b2117b6ef0e/image.png?part=0.2&amp;view=1\" alt=\"image.png\" width=\"478\" height=\"454\"><br><br><br></div></div>\r\n\r\n<p></p></blockquote></div>"
